Start();
include(utilities);
include(unitTests);
registry(fILEs);
define
    kantGrammar File='incant/kantGrammar';
    ;
include(kantGrammar);
search reset stack KantGrammar Grokking;
search Utilities UnitTests;
registry(cOMMANDs);
define
    testOP  immediateAction;
    ;
registry(ForOpT);
define
    qa=2;
    qb=3;
    fa=1;
    fb=2;
    fc=3;
    fsrc;
    it1;
    lp;
    foN code={
        for lp in fsrc;     print *lp:;
        for lp in <- fsrc;
            print *lp:;
            if *lp == 2;    break;
        print 8:;
        };
    nlReqN owner source result rule;
    foNew code={
        nlSrc := argument.CodE;
        nlReqN.owner  := argument;
        nlReqN.source := nlSrc;
        nlReqN.rule   := KantGrammar["STARt"];
        if compileIn(nlReqN);
            nlBlock = nlReqN.result;
            *nlBlock :. noPrinT;
            argument :. isActioN;
            argument +< *nlBlock;
        };
    foAct code={ for lp in <- fsrc; qa qb + 1; print "O-NEXT":; };
    ;
search ForOpT Utilities UnitTests list;
fsrc +% fa fb fc;
stop();

forOpT -- SEQ 594 (Tony, 2026-11-10): FOr split into fixed shapes. FOr becomes the parent of FORBACk (for .. in <- ..) and
FORWARd (for .. in ..), each an operator with the default action OPERATOr and its own op (forBack, forLoop); FOr's hand
action is deleted. The <- is a keyword that hands nothing up, so both rules hold cursor, source, statement. LOOPRESTRICt
(attributes / members) keeps today's refusal, given by its own action. forT and ruleForT's values must not move; these
rows pin what the split CHANGES and the controls it must not. Rows prefixed FO-. Interpreted only.

FO-1  the forward rule's statement slot refuses: today FOr's own wording; the default names FORWARd and the slot.
FO-2  the same with <-: the default names FORBACk.
FO-3  the reverse rule's source refuses: today the attach's refusal naming the internal list (forArms).
FO-4  a restriction, forward: today's wording stays, from LOOPRESTRICt's own action; the default then adds its line for
      the slot that handed up nothing.
FO-5  a restriction with <-: the same, the default's line naming FORBACk.
FO-6  control: the old road's For with <-, a refused body in an action: refused naming xl1, the rest does not run.
FO-7  control (R0): top level through FILe -- a forward loop, then a <- loop that breaks at 2, then a print.
FO-8  control (R0): the same statements as a new-road action body (foN, built by foNew -- whileT's whNew). A "parse
      failed" line would mean foN ran on the old road; the refusals column would show it.
FO-9  control: continue in a <- loop (3, then 1).

Not run alone: pop.sh appends the row, print "NEXT", a sentinel; one process per row. Each row reads exit and sentinel; stdout
(lines joined by |); and the lines that are REFUSED naming FOr, FORBACk, FORWARd, forArms or xl1, or say parse failed,
joined by |.

SEQ 596 R1 (Tony, 2026-11-10): one refusal per statement. The refusals column now holds EVERY REFUSED line of the run (and
any parse failed line), so it reads how many there are: today the first refusal and then the default action's line for the slot
that handed up nothing; intended the first refusal alone.

ROWS, separated by ^ -- id ^ statements ^ stdout today ^ refusals today ^ stdout intended ^ refusals intended
